        {
          "id": "what-is-l-carnitine",
          "level": 2,
          "heading": "What is L-Carnitine?",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "L-Carnitine is the naturally occurring L-form of carnitine, and levocarnitine is the same molecule under its pharmacopeial name. It is a small quaternary ammonium compound that cells build from lysine and methionine, and it is found across mammalian species (Reuter and Evans, 2012). It is a single small molecule, not a peptide: there is no amino acid chain and there are no peptide bonds."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Its best-known job is transport. Long-chain fatty acids cannot enter the mitochondrial matrix on their own, so carnitine carries their acyl groups across the inner membrane, where they can be broken down by β-oxidation. Without carnitine, those acyl groups cannot make the crossing. That relay is called the carnitine shuttle."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Researchers study carnitine because it sits where fatty-acid metabolism meets cellular energy. Heart and skeletal muscle lean heavily on fatty-acid oxidation, and they hold most of the body's carnitine (NIH ODS). The same molecule also anchors work on carnitine transporters and on the acylcarnitine profiles that laboratories measure to read metabolic state (Longo et al., 2016). That makes it a useful thread through several otherwise separate areas of metabolic research."
            }
          ]
        },
