        {
          "id": "what-does-l-carnitine-do",
          "level": 2,
          "heading": "What does L-Carnitine do?",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "text": "L-Carnitine participates in the transport of long-chain fatty acyl groups into mitochondria, where they can enter β-oxidation. Researchers therefore study carnitine in mitochondrial metabolism, fatty-acid oxidation, transport biology and related metabolic systems.",
              "label": "Short answer"
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Carnitine also helps cells handle excess acyl groups and keep free coenzyme A available (Longo et al., 2016; Reuter and Evans, 2012). That is the established biology."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "What happens when people take carnitine as a dietary supplement is a separate question. Results there are mixed and depend on the population, the form and the study design, so the evidence section treats the two separately."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "carnitine-shuttle",
          "level": 2,
          "heading": "The carnitine shuttle in brief",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "flow",
              "steps": [
                "Long-chain fatty acid",
                "Acyl-CoA",
                "Carnitine shuttle",
                "Mitochondrial matrix",
                "β-oxidation"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Mitochondria cannot import long-chain acyl-CoA directly, so the acyl group is handed to carnitine and carried across (Longo et al., 2016). Three proteins do the work:"
            },
            {
              "type": "ul",
              "items": [
                "CPT1, on the outer mitochondrial membrane, transfers the acyl group from coenzyme A to carnitine to form an acylcarnitine.",
                "CACT, the carnitine–acylcarnitine translocase, moves the acylcarnitine across the inner membrane in exchange for free carnitine.",
                "CPT2, on the inner membrane, hands the acyl group back to coenzyme A inside the matrix, ready for β-oxidation."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Free carnitine then returns to the cytoplasm through CACT for another round, while the acyl-CoA enters β-oxidation. Tissues that rely heavily on fatty-acid oxidation, such as heart and skeletal muscle, depend on this route (Longo et al., 2016)."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Medium- and short-chain fatty acids largely follow a different route. That is one reason simple one-line descriptions of carnitine miss most of the biochemistry."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "research-applications",
          "level": 2,
          "heading": "Where L-Carnitine is studied",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "These are research areas in which the molecule is studied, so they describe the science, not an outcome from Veracue material."
            },
            {
              "type": "cards",
              "items": [
                {
                  "title": "Mitochondrial metabolism",
                  "text": "Carnitine sits upstream of β-oxidation and oxidative phosphorylation. Acylcarnitine levels reflect the balance of acyl-CoA to free coenzyme A inside mitochondria, so the carnitine pool gives researchers one window onto mitochondrial function. Disturbances in mitochondrial metabolism can also show up in measured acylcarnitine profiles (Reuter and Evans, 2012)."
                },
                {
                  "title": "Fatty-acid oxidation",
                  "text": "Long-chain fatty-acid oxidation depends on the carnitine shuttle. Carnitine availability is therefore a natural variable in studies of substrate use, fasting metabolism and inherited defects of the pathway, while medium- and short-chain oxidation largely follows a different route. Researchers also examine how the shuttle is regulated, for example through malonyl-CoA inhibition of CPT1."
                },
                {
                  "title": "Carnitine transport",
                  "text": "Cells accumulate carnitine through the high-affinity OCTN2 transporter, encoded by SLC22A5. Transport assays, transporter variants and animal models of lost uptake all use carnitine as the substrate under study. Because sodium drives the uptake, and loss of OCTN2 function reduces intracellular carnitine, transporter biology is a research field of its own."
                },
                {
                  "title": "Skeletal muscle metabolism",
                  "text": "Skeletal muscle holds a large share of the body's carnitine and cannot finish its synthesis, so it relies on uptake. That makes muscle a natural setting for questions about carnitine content, fuel selection and exercise metabolism. Studies comparing vegetarians and omnivores, for example, have examined how muscle carnitine responds to supplementation."
                },
                {
                  "title": "Metabolic biochemistry",
                  "text": "Fatty-acid oxidation, glucose handling and insulin resistance are closely linked in muscle biology. Carnitine features in research on how these pathways interact and how they are measured, including glycemic markers in supplementation studies. The findings are mixed, however, and interpretation depends on the population and the measures used."
                },
                {
                  "title": "Acylcarnitine and metabolomics research",
                  "text": "Acylcarnitines are esters of carnitine with fatty acids of different chain lengths. Laboratories profile them by tandem mass spectrometry as markers of fatty-acid metabolism, reporting free carnitine as C0 and the esters by chain length. Because a profile reflects many pathways at once, it is widely used in metabolomics-related research."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
