          "id": "weight-loss-research",
          "level": 2,
          "heading": "L-Carnitine and Weight-Loss Research",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Some human trials and meta-analyses report small average weight differences between adults taking L-carnitine supplements and adults given placebo or control. The findings, however, vary from study to study, and they describe dietary supplementation, not a laboratory material."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Researchers looked at carnitine because it carries fatty acids into mitochondria for oxidation. That rationale is biochemical, so it does not by itself predict what supplementation does in people. In most studies, though, weight was a secondary outcome, and NIH ODS describes the overall results as equivocal."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "A 2016 meta-analysis reported an average difference of about 1.3 kg against control, and it found that the difference shrank with longer duration (Pooyandjoo et al., 2016). A larger 2020 analysis reported about 1.2 kg and called the effect modest. It found no significant change in waist circumference or body-fat percentage (Talenezhad et al., 2020)."
            },
            {
              "type": "p",
              "text": "Results also differ by population, form and study design, because participants have included adults with overweight, obesity and type 2 diabetes. NIH ODS concludes that larger studies are needed. None of this establishes an effect for Veracue material, and none of it is guidance for use."
            }
